<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahamatra</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mahamatras</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mahamatra was a title for senior officials in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a>. According to the empire's profile, they served as provincial governors and officials responsible for collecting revenue, maintaining law and order and supervising public works. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> also appointed special officers for spreading dhamma.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahamatra.jpg" alt="Mahamatra" width="320" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photograph:Eugen Hultzsch (died 1927) Transcription:Alexander Cunningham, Eugen Hultzsch, 1877 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dhamma_Mahaamaataa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The word means officer of high rank. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edicts-of-ashoka">Edicts of Ashoka</a>, inscribed on rocks and pillars in the reign of the Maurya emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> (269–233 BCE), mention Mahamatras repeatedly, and they appear to have been an essential part of his government: a class of senior officials in charge of various aspects of administration and justice. The edicts name groups with particular duties: Amta-mahamatras, in charge of foreigners, Stri-adhyaksha mahamatras, in charge of women, and the Dharma-Mahamatras, whose full title, Dhaṃma Mahāmātā, means inspectors of dharma.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>According to Major Rock Edict 5, there had been no Dharma-Mahamatras in the past; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> appointed them thirteen years after his coronation, in the fourteenth year of his reign (256 BCE). They worked among all religions to establish and promote dharma and for the welfare and happiness of all devoted to it, including among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, the Kambojas, the Gandharas, the Rastrikas, the Pitinikas and other peoples on the western borders.</p><p>Major Rock Edict 12 names them beside the Mahamatras in charge of the women's quarters and the officers of outlying areas, all working for growth in the essentials of all religions, which the king says he values more than gifts and honours. The 7th Major Pillar Edict, in E. Hultzsch's translation of 1925, records that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> set up pillars of morality, appointed Mahamatras of morality and issued proclamations on morality.</p><p>The same edict describes these officers as busy with affairs beneficial to ascetics and householders alike. Some were ordered to attend to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangha">Sangha</a>, others to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmanas">Brahmanas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajivika">Ajivikas</a>, others to the Nirgranthas and others to various sects, so that different Mahamatras dealt with different congregations, while all of them attended to every sect.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mahamatra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Government was centralised under the emperor, assisted by a council of ministers, while major provinces centred on Taxila, Ujjain, Tosali and Suvarnagiri were governed by royal princes (kumaras) as viceroys, supported by senior officials known as <b>Mahamatras</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
